#lang saffron
; saffron-dynamic-surface-pass (low): `=` on two strings through an `any`
; panicked "=: no operator for a cstr argument" on BOTH back ends, while bool
; and numeric `=` answered. String equality is table stakes for a dynamic
; dialect, and in Saffron -- where every value is an `any` -- there is no other
; spelling for it.
;
; The `=` OPERATOR genuinely has no cstr row anywhere, typed Turmeric included:
; `(= "ab" "ab")` in a plain .tur file is a TUR-E0006 operator-lookup error,
; with `cstr-eq?` or `(.eq? a b)` as the named alternatives. So this is NOT a
; missing builtin row being routed around -- adding one would change the typed
; surface. It is the DYNAMIC path answering what `Eq[cstr]`
; (stdlib/typeclass-eq.tur) answers, exactly as H5 made dynamic `=` on two
; Syms answer what `Eq[Sym]` answers though `=` has no Sym row either.
;
; Semantics are `cstr-eq?`'s, documented there and matched byte for byte on
; both back ends: both-NULL equal, a NULL never equal to a non-NULL (including
; the empty string), otherwise strcmp.
;
; The two back ends reached the refusal at DIFFERENT sites, which is why the
; fix is two arms rather than one: compiled, `__tur_dyn_cmp` fell through to
; its numeric guard; interpreted, a cstr is a SCALAR any-box carrying no struct
; name, so it slipped past the named-box refusal and died on the failed
; `builtin_lookup` instead.
;
; The last line pins the other half: ORDERING and MIXED-type compares still
; refuse, in the same words, on both back ends -- so this arm did not quietly
; become "compare anything".

(defn t [x] x)

(defn main []
  (println (= (t "ab") (t "ab")))
  (println (= (t "ab") (t "cd")))
  (println (not= (t "ab") (t "cd")))
  (println (= (t "") (t "")))
  (println (= (t "ab") (t 1)))
  0)
